WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.390
(bells jingling)

00:00:15.948 --> 00:00:18.219
- [Woman] Have you ever
wondered if you watched

00:00:18.219 --> 00:00:22.302
the snow long enough, what
stories it might tell?

00:00:24.082 --> 00:00:26.674
There is someone who has done it.

00:00:26.674 --> 00:00:29.365
His name is Billy Barr.

00:00:29.365 --> 00:00:31.115
- [Billy] I spell it,

00:00:34.406 --> 00:00:37.091
- [Woman] Some people call
him the Snow Guardian.

00:00:37.091 --> 00:00:39.976
He lives in a cabin out in the woods.

00:00:39.976 --> 00:00:43.449
- Picture this, it's a snowy
day, it's dark and cold,

00:00:43.449 --> 00:00:46.224
and you make a fire and
you're sitting by the fire

00:00:46.224 --> 00:00:48.306
and you're reading with a cup of tea,

00:00:48.306 --> 00:00:50.889
and it goes on for nine months.

00:00:52.492 --> 00:00:56.659
- [Woman] Billy lives alone
in his house he helped build.

00:00:58.015 --> 00:00:59.838
Here he grows his garden.

00:00:59.838 --> 00:01:02.697
Has an impressive hat collection.

00:01:02.697 --> 00:01:04.411
Loves cricket,

00:01:04.411 --> 00:01:06.411
and dreams of Bollywood.

00:01:08.322 --> 00:01:10.828
Every couple of weeks, he skis back

00:01:10.828 --> 00:01:13.492
into the nearest town for supplies.

00:01:13.492 --> 00:01:17.325
He's been doing this for
more than 40 winters.

00:01:18.673 --> 00:01:23.166
But Billy does a little more
than just read and drink tea.

00:01:23.166 --> 00:01:27.751
For those 40 winters, Billy
has kept a meticulous record

00:01:27.751 --> 00:01:30.926
of snow in his little part of the world.

00:01:30.926 --> 00:01:32.228
- Okay, the market's at...

00:01:32.228 --> 00:01:36.745
February 26th, 1978: 10.5
inches of snow that day;

00:01:36.745 --> 00:01:38.889
January 20th, minus 11.5;

00:01:38.889 --> 00:01:43.056
April 28th, 1980, I was
41 during that snow storm.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:48.583
1997, one-half inch new
snow, a weasel was roaming

00:01:48.583 --> 00:01:50.642
around inside the shack.

00:01:50.642 --> 00:01:52.809
Damn, the birds were back.

00:01:55.641 --> 00:01:59.808
I lived in an 8x10 foot, old
shack that had no electricity,

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:04.002
no water, and I had nothing
and I was just there all day.

00:02:04.002 --> 00:02:08.532
The main thing I interacted
with was the weather

00:02:08.532 --> 00:02:10.133
and the animals.

00:02:10.133 --> 00:02:11.558
So I started recording things

00:02:11.558 --> 00:02:14.475
just 'cause it was something to do.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:18.481
I had nothing to prove,
no goals, no anything.

00:02:18.481 --> 00:02:21.483
So, actually a researcher at
a lab wanted to look at it,

00:02:21.483 --> 00:02:25.025
and then once he started looking
at it scientifically,

00:02:25.025 --> 00:02:27.963
then all of a sudden these
decades worth of data

00:02:27.963 --> 00:02:31.880
were being used for more
than my own curiosity.

00:02:39.418 --> 00:02:42.081
- [Woman] Billy has done
this everyday, twice a day,

00:02:42.081 --> 00:02:43.414
all winter long.

00:02:46.801 --> 00:02:48.763
- I'd keep going until the snow was gone.

00:02:48.763 --> 00:02:52.513
If it snowed, I'd record
that no matter when.

00:02:59.534 --> 00:03:02.406
The trend I see is that we're
getting a permanent snow

00:03:02.406 --> 00:03:06.325
pack later and we get
to bare ground sooner.

00:03:06.325 --> 00:03:08.826
With how the years were,
there was a lot of snow

00:03:08.826 --> 00:03:12.301
on the ground and then we
lost snow sooner than years

00:03:12.301 --> 00:03:16.002
that had a lot less snow just
because it's a lot warmer now.

00:03:16.002 --> 00:03:18.127
- [Woman] In a normal
winter, you'd expect to have

00:03:18.127 --> 00:03:21.137
four to five record high temperatures.

00:03:21.137 --> 00:03:23.554
Last year, Billy recorded 36.

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:26.941
- Not only is it a lot
warmer, we're getting

00:03:26.941 --> 00:03:28.965
a lot of dust blowing in.

00:03:28.965 --> 00:03:32.522
As soon as you get dust on
the snow, it melts like that.

00:03:32.522 --> 00:03:36.994
You're talking about the
snow pack, the water supply

00:03:36.994 --> 00:03:39.161
for most of the southwest.

00:03:40.804 --> 00:03:43.804
I'm not real hopeful just because...

00:03:46.562 --> 00:03:50.645
I don't know how you
reverse something like that.

00:03:56.464 --> 00:03:58.883
- [Woman] As we leave Colorado behind,

00:03:58.883 --> 00:04:02.542
Billy imparts once last bit of advice.

00:04:02.542 --> 00:04:06.846
- It's like anything else, I
learned to ski to get around,

00:04:06.846 --> 00:04:09.558
I learned how to ski
better so I wouldn't fall

00:04:09.558 --> 00:04:11.683
down all the time.

00:04:11.683 --> 00:04:13.726
Over a period of time I kinda learned

00:04:13.726 --> 00:04:16.643
how to survive in this environment.

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:19.934
Actually, learning to fall is probably

00:04:19.934 --> 00:04:22.288
the most important thing.

00:04:22.288 --> 00:04:23.709
If you're gonna fall, sit.

00:04:23.709 --> 00:04:28.352
A lot easier falling on
your butt than on your face.

00:04:28.352 --> 00:04:30.935
(somber music)

00:04:52.062 --> 00:04:54.729
(musical chime)

